WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.370
In the spring of 2018, teachers unions
had a wave of victories with a string of red

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:10.500
state strikes, but summer brought a major
defeat when the Supreme Court ruled

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.360
in Janus v. AFSCME that agency fees are unconstitutional.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:28.720
Before Janus, some states compelled non-union
teachers to pay an agency fee to the union

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.140
that represented them in contract negotiations.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:38.320
Agency fees have kept teachers union membership,
and revenue, high in these states.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.840
Unions have anticipated this verdict.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:46.880
In the words of one union president, they
are “preparing to become voluntary organizations.”

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.040
In advance of Janus, the NEA projected significant
losses over two years,

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.540
predominantly in agency fee states.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.680
Well... former agency fee states.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:03.500
Big losses after Janus could easily be a game-changer
for national teachers unions, which are among

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:08.229
the most powerful interest groups in American
politics, and easily the most powerful on

00:01:08.229 --> 00:01:09.229
education issues.

00:01:09.229 --> 00:01:13.299
A tight bond with the Democratic Party has
worked for unions.

00:01:13.299 --> 00:01:18.979
It's been pretty effective blocking unwanted
education reforms, and in blocking

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:22.350
attacks on the linchpins of union power, like
agency fees.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:28.440
However, the blocking that has worked well in legislatures, couldn't stop the Supreme Court.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.159
Now, unions will have to go on offense.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:39.160
But now playing offense will be even harder, because to prove their value to teachers after Janus,

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.760
unions will have to do more with less.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.340
One path forward is to close ranks, as

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.840
in New York, where unions pushed a
state law that would bar non-members from

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:50.670
receiving full union benefits.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:54.280
Fair enough, but closing ranks may not be
the best bet.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:59.020
At loggerheads with the state and national
NEA over political endorsements, Nevada’s

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:04.500
largest local took a radical step in
2018 of disaffiliating from both, and

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.399
the losses were staggering.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.760
Perhaps going on offense will avoid such losses
if unions can build a bigger tent for members;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.620
one that is focused on local teachers’ concerns first
and less on remote political concerns.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.480
But what about the red state strike strategy?

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:26.080
That strategy caught fire in 
non-agency fee states.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.790
Could that be the unions' answer after Janus?

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:34.680
Maybe, but it will be a tough strategy to
bring to the states long used to agency fees.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:39.560
The red state strikes stood out, in part,
because they were the first statewide strikes

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:41.209
since 1990.

00:02:41.209 --> 00:02:43.260
But they stood out for other key reasons.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:49.849
First, they all had legitimate statewide complaints
to rally around, like statewide salary schedules,

00:02:49.849 --> 00:02:52.260
and markedly low teacher pay.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:58.020
But only 15 states set teacher pay at the
state level, and three of them just went on strike.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:04.000
So for most teachers, the key rallying point is a district issue, not a state issue.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.080
Those strikes also confronted low state education
spending, but that isn’t markedly low across

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:10.580
the country, either.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:16.319
Another distinction: These strikes were spurred less by unions, which were relatively weak in these states,

00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:20.030
and more by
local teachers who started Facebook groups

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:23.709
that quickly grew to tens of thousands of
members.

00:03:23.709 --> 00:03:28.590
The solidarity that galvanized these grassroots
movements of “teachers next door” will

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:34.069
be harder to come by for unions easily pegged
as special interests.

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:39.459
And here is where unions in agency fee states
may prove the victims of their own successes.

00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:45.540
Agency-fee states tend to have higher salaries
and education funding, leaving them without

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.340
salient issues for teachers to rally around.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:52.800
Unions will have a hard time using the red
state strike playbook in the very states where

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:56.360
they expect the heaviest losses.

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:58.430
So it sounds like unions are dead, huh?

00:03:58.430 --> 00:03:59.510
Well, not quite.

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:03.140
They'll be smaller. The question is if they'll be a lot different.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:07.080
While hard times may be coming at the state
and national levels, Janus doesn’t

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:12.320
alter the basic reasons teachers join
unions: to organize around their common interests

00:04:12.329 --> 00:04:14.940
and have a seat at the local bargaining table.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:19.300
So the most pertinent question may be what
will change at the local level,

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.940
particularly in agency fee states.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:26.820
Most locals will get smaller, but smaller
unions can take a few different forms.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:29.060
Here's four possibilities.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:34.860
First: Many will lose their least committed members,
leaving their more ardent supporters

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.340
to push for more strident positions.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:43.870
For instance, groups like the Bad-Ass Teacher
Association push locals to be more confrontational.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:50.460
Where such groups become dominant, expect
less stable, and more contentious, education politics.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:56.460
Second: Many shrinking locals won’t become more
strident—but may go dormant—until new

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:00.720
adverse state or district policies awaken a
resurgence.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.340
That’s a likely pattern in agency
fee states accustomed to bargaining advantages,

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.340
that policymakers may
now aim to pare down.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:14.270
However, this pattern could push unions once
powered by agency fees to refocus squarely

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:16.560
on local issues.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:22.200
Third: Other locals could go for a bigger tent. Taking a page from the red state strike playbook,

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:27.760
some locals could shed the strong association with union headquarters and Democratic party priorities.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:32.940
They could remove party lines to attract a broader coalition of active members.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:37.420
That route wouldn’t make local politics
less contentious, but it would center them

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:42.560
on local teachers’ shared priorities, potentially
leading to more representative,

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.240
and more durable, local unions.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.160
Finally, some locals might just fade away.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:52.860
The red state strikes suggest that today there are alternative means of organizing

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:56.560
which could compete for the right to exclusively speak for teachers.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:03.580
Janus could crack the virtual monopoly unions have representing one of the nation's largest workforces.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:08.960
School districts will have to bargain, and teachers will be represented on the other side of the table.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:14.320
How unions evolve, and whether new entrants come on the scene, only time will tell.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:19.920
But Janus will amount to a historic change in how teachers organize.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:24.240
What do you think will happen to teachers unions after the Janus verdict?

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:29.300
